  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Let's call a positive integer MMM a beautiful number if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n\nThe decimal representation of MMM (without leading zeros) does not contain any zeros.\nThe sum of squares of all decimal digits of MMM is a perfect square.\nFor example, 111, 444 and 221221221 are beautiful, but 101010 and 333333333 are not.\n\nYou are given a positive integer NNN. Find the smallest NNN-digit beautiful number or determine that all NNN-digit integers are not beautiful.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1001 \\le T \\le 1001≤T≤100",
      "1≤N≤1061 \\le N \\le 10^61≤N≤106",
      "the sum of NNN over all test cases does not exceed 10610^6106",
      "Subtasks**Subtask #1 (10 points):** 1≤N≤1001 \\le N \\le 1001≤N≤100",
      "**Subtask #2 (90 points):** 1≤T≤101 \\le T \\le 101≤T≤10"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "The first line of the input contains a single integer TTT denoting the number of test cases. The description of TTT test cases follows.\nThe first and only line of each test case contains a single integer NNN.",
    "outputFormat": "For each test case, print a single line containing one integer ― the smallest NNN-digit beautiful number, or −1-1−1 if there is no such number.",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "2\n1\n2",
        "output": "1\n34",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nExample case 1: It is obvious that 111 is the smallest beautiful number.\nExample case 2: 343434 is beautiful because 32+42=9+16=25=523^2 + 4^2 = 9 + 16 = 25 = 5^232+42=9+16=25=52. On the other hand, we can prove that this is the smallest beautiful number."
      }
    ]
  },
